         <article class="section">
+            <h2 class="section-title">Product pillars (2026)</h2>
+            <p class="card-desc">
+                Beyond the core loop (connect a Mac, send a Task, watch a Run, save a Playbook), Agent Witch is built around four promises: <strong>easy authoring</strong> for people who are not prompt engineers; <strong>learn from usage</strong> with human approval before playbooks change; <strong>efficient memory</strong> so new tasks reuse useful context from past runs; and <strong>team learning</strong> through shared Reports, Playbooks, and company templates—not isolated chat threads.
+            </p>
+
             <h2 class="section-title">The Problem</h2>
             <p class="card-desc">
                 Most AI coding agents run wherever the IDE happens to be open. That's fine for a solo developer, but it breaks down for a team: there's no shared run history, no way to dispatch a task to a teammate's machine, no audit trail, and no policy layer over who can run what. Agent Witch exists to give a team a single place to trigger trusted agent runs on real hardware &mdash; a Mac they own, not a shared cloud sandbox &mdash; with the same rigor you'd expect from any internal platform: authentication, approvals, and a record of what happened.
